\section{Generating QR artifacts}

The QR output should be readable from a projected slide, so we keep the chosen
error correction level and pixel scale as named constants.

<<constants>>=
QR_ERROR_LEVEL = "m"
QR_SCALE = 10
@

The single public function does three things: ensure the parent directory
exists, write the QR image, with [[segno]] inferring the output format from the
extension, and write the [[.pdf_tex]] sidecar next to it.  We write the sidecar
for every format because LaTeX-facing consumers (slides and the printed manual)
may prefer either PDF or PNG output, and the sidecar is cheap to produce.  The
HTML results page consumes the image file directly via [[<img>]] and simply
ignores the sidecar---an extra small text file on disk is far less of a hazard
than silently producing a broken [[\input]] target.

Regeneration adds one more constraint.  The server should be free to refresh a
QR image by absolute filesystem path even if it runs from another working
directory, but the sidecar's [[\includegraphics]] line still needs the original
LaTeX-facing path the document source used.  We therefore let callers override
the path written into the sidecar while keeping the on-disk output path for the
actual image write.

<<functions>>=
def make_qr(
    url: str,
    out_path: str | Path,
    fmt: str = "pdf",
    *,
    include_path: str | Path | None = None,
) -> Path:
    """Write a QR code for ``url`` to ``out_path`` and return the path.

    A ``.pdf_tex`` sidecar is written next to the image regardless of
    the chosen format.  Slides and printed handouts that ``\\input``
    the sidecar render the QR matrix and the URL caption together, so
    the two cannot drift apart even when the QR file is later
    regenerated for a new base URL.

    ``include_path`` lets callers keep the sidecar's LaTeX graphics path
    stable even when ``out_path`` is an absolute filesystem path used for
    runtime regeneration.
    """

    path = Path(out_path)
    target = path if path.suffix else path.with_suffix(f".{fmt}")
    target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    _write_qr_image(url, target)
    _write_pdf_tex_sidecar(target, url, include_path=include_path)
    return target
@

\subsection{Writing the image whole}

Slides and notes built by [[make -j]] share a store and so write the same QR
files at the same moment, and a server start can join them.  Writing in place
loses data there.  One writer's [[open]] empties the file that the other has
just finished, and a writer that then reads the file back to blank its date, as
[[make_qr]] used to do, writes those zero bytes over it.  Two processes started
together on one target left an empty file in about one round in a thousand, and
[[pdflatex]] stopped with [[reading image file failed]].  PythonTeX does not
rerun an unchanged block, so nothing repaired it.  A [[pdflatex]] reading the
file during a write could see the same empty or partial file.

So [[segno]] writes a temporary file only this call knows about, the blanking
happens there, and [[os.replace]] moves the finished file into place.  The move
is atomic: a reader finds the earlier file or the new one, and two writers can
only overwrite each other with a whole file.

The temporary file is a path rather than an [[io.BytesIO]], for two reasons.
[[segno]] cannot write EPS, TeX, XBM or XPM to a binary stream.  And since it
opens the path with a plain [[open]], the umask decides the mode just as when it
wrote the target directly, whereas [[tempfile]] would make the file 0600 and
lock colleagues out of a shared deck directory.  A file that already exists
hands on its mode, since writing in place would have kept that too.

The name ends with the target's, because [[segno]] takes the format from it, and
starts with a dot, so that a shell pattern like [[Q-*.pdf]] does not match it.
A random part keeps writers apart.  The store's process number would not do
here, since threads of one process write QR files too, and a deck directory can
be on a file system shared between machines.  The price is that a process killed
before the move leaves its file behind for good, while a process number would
let a later process recognise it.  That window is short, and the file is small
and harmless.

The target's symbolic links are resolved first, as [[os.replace]] would replace
the link instead of writing through it.  Replacing does need one permission that
writing in place did not, namely to write in the directory.

<<imports>>=
import contextlib
import os
import secrets
import shutil
<<functions>>=
def _write_qr_image(url: str, target: Path):
    destination = Path(os.path.realpath(target))
    temporary = destination.with_name(
        f".{secrets.token_hex(8)}.{destination.name}"
    )
    try:
        segno.make(url, error=QR_ERROR_LEVEL).save(temporary, scale=QR_SCALE)
        _blank_pdf_creation_date(temporary)
        with contextlib.suppress(FileNotFoundError):
            shutil.copymode(destination, temporary)
        os.replace(temporary, destination)
    finally:
        temporary.unlink(missing_ok=True)
@

\subsection{Blanking the PDF's date}

The same URL should give the same bytes, and a PDF from [[segno]] does not: it
carries a [[/CreationDate]] with the time it was written, and [[segno]] has no
option to leave it out.  That matters because [[latexmk]] decides by content
whether an input has changed.  We tried it: rewriting a QR PDF for the same URL
made [[latexmk]] compile the document again, where nothing had changed.  Every
server start and every PythonTeX run rewrites these files, so the date alone
would make the next [[latexmk]] run compile again.  Nobody reads the date of a
QR code, and the file's own timestamp already says when it was written.

We blank the date with spaces rather than cut it out.  The PDF's cross-reference
table records byte offsets, and the [[startxref]] offset lies after the date, so
removing the bytes would leave the file pointing at the wrong place.  Spaces
between two dictionary entries are valid PDF and keep every offset right.  A
later [[segno]] that writes the date differently would slip past the pattern.
Comparing two writes need not notice that, as they agree within one second, so
the offsets test below also looks for the date's key in the file itself.

Only PDF needs this.  PNG and SVG carry no date, and we leave the date in the
EPS and TeX formats, which give different bytes at each write: [[pdflatex]]
cannot include EPS, and the slides use neither.

<<imports>>=
import re
<<constants>>=
PDF_CREATION_DATE = re.compile(rb"/CreationDate\(D:[^)]*\)")
<<functions>>=
def _blank_pdf_creation_date(image: Path):
    if image.suffix.lower() != ".pdf":
        return
    image.write_bytes(
        PDF_CREATION_DATE.sub(
            lambda date: b" " * len(date[0]), image.read_bytes()
        )
    )
@

\section{The [[.pdf_tex]] sidecar}
\label{sec:qr-sidecar}

The sidecar is a small LaTeX snippet rather than another image.  Its body
[[\includegraphics]]es the QR PDF and then prints the URL beneath it as
ordinary text.  Two small invariants make this robust.

First, the [[\includegraphics]] path inside the sidecar matches the path the
caller wants LaTeX to see.  This matters because LaTeX does not adjust the
graphics search path when [[\input]] reads a file: a bare basename inside the
sidecar would be looked up relative to the calling document, not the sidecar's
own directory.  Echoing the caller's LaTeX-facing path means the sidecar
resolves the QR file from the same working directory that the slide compile
uses---which is exactly what a hand-written [[\includegraphics]] line would
have done.

Second, the sidecar inherits its width from its caller via [[\linewidth]].
Slide and handout layouts already place the QR inside a column or an inner
minipage, so the sidecar's [[\linewidth]] tracks whatever width the caller has
chosen.  This avoids the [[\svgwidth]]/[[\svgscale]] dance Inkscape uses for
its sidecars: we do not need a scale-override knob in this codebase, and
adding one would just be one more thing for callers to forget to set.

The caption is not typeset directly but handed to a macro,
[[\mentipyqrcaption]], whose default is [[\footnotesize\ttfamily]]: monospace
at a size that fits beneath a slide-sized QR without crowding the matrix.
The default is declared with [[\providecommand]] inside the sidecar itself,
so a document that says nothing gets the default, while a document that
defines [[\mentipyqrcaption]] before its first [[\input]] wins.  The hook has
to live in the sidecar rather than in the document because the caption text
is the one thing [[regenerate_qrs]] rewrites: a document that wants a
smaller or breakable URL (a narrow Beamer column, say) must be able to
change the \emph{formatting} without copying the \emph{content} out of the
sidecar, since any copy is frozen at compile time and stops following the
server.  Repeating the [[\providecommand]] in every sidecar is harmless:
after the first definition it is a no-op.

<<functions>>=
def _write_pdf_tex_sidecar(
    pdf_path: Path,
    url: str,
    *,
    include_path: str | Path | None = None,
) -> Path:
    """Write the ``.pdf_tex`` companion next to ``pdf_path``."""

    sidecar = pdf_path.with_suffix(QR_TEX_SUFFIX)
    text = _sidecar_text(pdf_path, url, include_path)
    try:
        current = sidecar.read_text()
    except (OSError, UnicodeError):
        current = None
    if current != text:
        sidecar.write_text(text)
    return sidecar


def _sidecar_text(
    pdf_path: Path, url: str, include_path: str | Path | None
) -> str:
    include_target = Path(include_path) if include_path is not None else pdf_path
    return (
        "%% Generated by mentipy. Do not edit.\n"
        "\\begingroup%\n"
        "\\begin{minipage}{\\linewidth}%\n"
        "\\centering%\n"
        f"{SIDECAR_INCLUDE_PREFIX}{include_target.as_posix()}}}%\n"
        "\\par\\smallskip\n"
        f"{QR_CAPTION_DEFAULT}%\n"
        f"\\mentipyqrcaption{{{_escape_url_for_tex(url)}}}%\n"
        "\\end{minipage}%\n"
        "\\endgroup%\n"
    )
@

A sidecar whose text would not change keeps its file, and so its date.  Deck
Makefiles depend on the sidecars, so a new date means a rebuild of the slides, and
both the server and every PythonTeX run write these files with nothing to change
most of the time.  The image is always written, though, since a matching sidecar
says nothing about it: [[Q-x.pdf]], [[Q-x.png]] and [[Q-x.svg]] in one directory
share one sidecar, so it can match while another format still holds an old address,
and an interrupted write leaves the two disagreeing as well.  Writing the image on
every call repairs that for each target the caller names, and a server start names
every remembered one.  This relies on Makefiles depending on the sidecars rather
than on the images, whose dates do move; a direct [[latexmk]] run compares content,
and the image's bytes stay the same.  The one case it misses is narrow: an image
repaired at the same address leaves the sidecar's date alone, so [[make]] does not
rebuild a PDF made from the broken image, though the server still says to
recompile.  Here a sidecar that cannot be read counts as different and gets
overwritten, as it always did in [[make_qr]]; a server start still fails on one
earlier, when [[sidecar_include_path]] reads it.

<<constants>>=
QR_TEX_SUFFIX = ".pdf_tex"
SIDECAR_INCLUDE_PREFIX = "\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{"
QR_CAPTION_DEFAULT = (
    "\\providecommand{\\mentipyqrcaption}[1]{{\\footnotesize\\ttfamily #1}}"
)
@

\subsection{Escaping the URL}

URLs in real polls regularly carry characters that LaTeX text mode treats as
special: underscores in short prefixes, [[%]] and [[&]] in query strings,
[[#]] in fragments.  Without escaping, the caption either fails to compile or
silently swallows characters.  We use the same escape table as
[[mentipy.latex]]; duplicating it locally avoids an import cycle (the LaTeX
helpers import [[make_qr]] from this module) and keeps the QR module
self-contained.

<<functions>>=
def _escape_url_for_tex(url: str) -> str:
    return "".join(_TEX_ESCAPES.get(char, char) for char in url)
@

<<constants>>=
_TEX_ESCAPES = {
    "\\": r"\textbackslash{}",
    "&": r"\&",
    "%": r"\%",
    "$": r"\$",
    "#": r"\#",
    "_": r"\_",
    "{": r"\{",
    "}": r"\}",
    "~": r"\textasciitilde{}",
    "^": r"\textasciicircum{}",
}
@

\subsection{Reading the include path back}

The include path inside the sidecar is the one piece of the sidecar that
only the original render knows: it is the path as the document author
wrote it, relative to wherever that document compiles.  When
[[mentipy serve]] later rewrites the sidecar for a new URL, it knows
only the absolute path of the QR file, and any attempt to recompute the
author's relative path from its own working directory guesses wrong as
soon as the server runs somewhere else --- say, from the directory of a
book that binds the deck, or from the home directory.  The sidecar on
disk already records the right answer, so the rewrite reads it back
instead of recomputing it.  Parsing our own output is safe here because
the same [[SIDECAR_INCLUDE_PREFIX]] constant writes and reads the line.

<<functions>>=
def sidecar_include_path(qr_path: str | Path) -> Path | None:
    """Return the graphics path recorded in the sidecar next to `qr_path`.

    Returns `None` when the sidecar is missing or was not written by
    Mentipy, so callers can fall back to computing a path themselves.
    """

    sidecar = Path(qr_path).with_suffix(QR_TEX_SUFFIX)
    try:
        lines = sidecar.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    for line in lines:
        if line.startswith(SIDECAR_INCLUDE_PREFIX) and line.endswith("}%"):
            return Path(line[len(SIDECAR_INCLUDE_PREFIX):-2])
    return None
@

\section{QR artifact contracts}

The first downstream dependency is mundane but important: LaTeX and browsers
expect an actual file, not merely a successful return value.  Checking the PDF
header catches regressions where [[segno]] was called with the wrong target or
where the parent-directory setup silently failed.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_writes_a_pdf(tmp_path):
    output = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")

    assert output.exists()
    assert output.stat().st_size > 0
    assert output.read_bytes()[:5] == b"%PDF-"
@

Regeneration may run several times for the same question as the public address
changes or the document is rebuilt.  For a fixed URL, however, the formats the
slides and pages use should be stable, whatever the time; otherwise a rebuild
alone would create needless file churn.  The test moves the clock between the
two writes instead of waiting for it: two writes in one second would agree even
with a date in the file.  The clock is the one thing here we cannot make real,
and it is [[segno]]'s present clock only, so this test cannot see a date from
any other source.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("fmt", ["pdf", "png", "svg"])
def test_make_qr_is_deterministic_for_the_same_url(tmp_path, monkeypatch, fmt):
    url = "http://localhost:8080/abcd"
    first = make_qr(url, tmp_path / f"first.{fmt}", fmt=fmt)
    monkeypatch.setattr("time.strftime", lambda *_args: "20300101000000")
    second = make_qr(url, tmp_path / f"second.{fmt}", fmt=fmt)

    assert first.read_bytes() == second.read_bytes()
@

Blanking must leave a PDF that a reader can still follow.  The cross-reference
table has to sit where [[startxref]] says, and each entry in it has to point at
its object, which fails if the blanking ever changes the length of the file.
Whatever replaces the date has to be white space, as anything else corrupts the
dictionary, so the test checks that only white space separates the start of the
dictionary from its [[/Producer]].  And the key must be gone: that is what
notices a date written in a way the pattern misses, whichever clock it comes
from.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_keeps_the_pdf_offsets_right(tmp_path):
    pdf = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q.pdf").read_bytes()

    [table] = re.findall(rb"startxref\r\n(\d+)", pdf)
    assert pdf[int(table):].startswith(b"xref")
    entries = re.findall(rb"(\d{10}) 00000 n", pdf)
    assert len(entries) == 5
    for number, offset in enumerate(entries, start=1):
        assert pdf[int(offset):].startswith(b"%d 0 obj" % number)
    assert b"CreationDate" not in pdf
    assert re.search(rb"5 0 obj <<\s+/Producer\(", pdf)
@

The format comes from the extension whatever its case, so the date goes there
too.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_blanks_the_date_whatever_the_extension_looks_like(tmp_path):
    pdf = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q.PDF").read_bytes()

    assert b"CreationDate" not in pdf
@

\subsection{A whole file, whatever the timing}

Each test below holds one property of the replacement.  The first two are the
race itself.  Its timing cannot be had on demand from real processes, so the
first test puts the other writer's truncation at the worst moment, right after
[[segno]] has written; the second needs no such help, since a reader that
opened the file before the write is real, and in-place writing changes what it
reads.

<<test functions>>=
URL = "http://localhost:8080/abcd"


def _after_segno_writes(monkeypatch, action):
    real_save = segno.QRCode.save

    def save_and_act(self, out, **kwargs):
        real_save(self, out, **kwargs)
        action()

    monkeypatch.setattr(segno.QRCode, "save", save_and_act)


def test_make_qr_survives_another_writer_emptying_the_file(
    tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
    whole = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "whole.pdf").read_bytes()
    target = tmp_path / "Q.pdf"
    _after_segno_writes(monkeypatch, lambda: target.write_bytes(b""))

    make_qr(URL, target)

    assert target.read_bytes() == whole


def test_make_qr_leaves_the_file_a_reader_has_open_alone(tmp_path):
    target = make_qr("http://old.example/abcd", tmp_path / "Q.pdf")
    earlier = target.read_bytes()

    with target.open("rb") as reader:
        make_qr("http://new.example/abcd", target)

        assert reader.read() == earlier
    assert target.read_bytes() != earlier
@

The second test cannot see a moment with no file at the target, as a delete
and write would make, so here is the same promise under real concurrency.  Two
threads write while the main thread reads.  This can only pass by luck on broken
code, never fail by luck on working code: whatever the timing, every read of
correct code gets the whole file.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_shows_a_concurrent_reader_only_whole_files(tmp_path):
    target = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "Q.pdf")
    whole = target.read_bytes()

    def keep_writing():
        for _ in range(100):
            make_qr(URL, target)

    with ThreadPoolExecutor(2) as pool:
        writers = [pool.submit(keep_writing) for _ in range(2)]
        seen = {target.read_bytes()}
        while not all(writer.done() for writer in writers):
            seen.add(target.read_bytes())
    for writer in writers:
        writer.result()

    assert seen == {whole}
@

Two writers in one process must not share a temporary file, which the process
number alone would make them do.  The barrier holds both after [[segno]] has
written, so with one shared name the file can only be moved once and the other
writer fails.  Its limit only keeps a bug from hanging the suite.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_writers_in_one_process_keep_apart(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    target = tmp_path / "Q.pdf"
    whole = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "whole.pdf").read_bytes()
    both_have_written = threading.Barrier(2, timeout=300)
    _after_segno_writes(monkeypatch, both_have_written.wait)

    with ThreadPoolExecutor(2) as pool:
        writers = [pool.submit(make_qr, URL, target) for _ in range(2)]
    for writer in writers:
        writer.result()

    assert target.read_bytes() == whole
    assert _names_in(tmp_path) == {
        "Q.pdf", "Q.pdf_tex", "whole.pdf", "whole.pdf_tex"
    }


def _names_in(directory):
    return {path.name for path in directory.iterdir()}
@

While it exists, the temporary file stays out of the way of a Makefile's or a
shell's wildcard, which might otherwise pick it up as one more QR code.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_hides_its_temporary_file_from_wildcards(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    found = []
    _after_segno_writes(
        monkeypatch,
        lambda: found.extend(glob.glob(str(tmp_path / "*.pdf"))),
    )

    make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "Q.pdf")

    assert found == []
    assert (tmp_path / "Q.pdf").exists()
@

A QR file keeps the permissions it would have had when written in place: a new
one follows the umask, which the temporary files of [[tempfile]] do not, and an
existing one keeps its mode.  In a directory shared with colleagues, 0600 would
lock them out of the deck's QR codes.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_gives_a_new_file_the_mode_of_a_plain_open(tmp_path):
    earlier = os.umask(0o027)
    try:
        target = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "Q.pdf")
    finally:
        os.umask(earlier)

    assert stat.S_IMODE(target.stat().st_mode) == 0o640


def test_make_qr_keeps_the_mode_of_an_existing_file(tmp_path):
    target = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "Q.pdf")
    target.chmod(0o640)

    make_qr("http://other.example/abcd", target)

    assert stat.S_IMODE(target.stat().st_mode) == 0o640
@

A symbolic link at the target stays a link, and the file it points to gets the
new code, as with any write through a link.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_writes_through_a_symbolic_link(tmp_path):
    real = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "elsewhere" / "Q.pdf")
    earlier = real.read_bytes()
    link = tmp_path / "Q.pdf"
    link.symlink_to(real)

    make_qr("http://other.example/abcd", link)

    assert link.is_symlink()
    assert real.read_bytes() != earlier
@

A write that fails leaves the earlier file as it was and no temporary file
behind.  Limiting the size of the files this process may write makes the write
itself fail for real, like a full disk, and a directory at the target makes the
last step fail.  The limit is not there on every platform.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_keeps_the_earlier_file_when_the_write_fails(tmp_path):
    resource = pytest.importorskip("resource")
    target = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / "Q.pdf")
    earlier = target.read_bytes()
    names = _names_in(tmp_path)
    soft, hard = resource.getrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE)

    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (100, hard))
    try:
        with pytest.raises(OSError):
            make_qr("http://other.example/abcd", target)
    finally:
        resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_FSIZE, (soft, hard))

    assert target.read_bytes() == earlier
    assert _names_in(tmp_path) == names


def test_make_qr_removes_its_temporary_file_when_the_last_step_fails(tmp_path):
    target = tmp_path / "Q.pdf"
    target.mkdir()

    with pytest.raises(OSError):
        make_qr(URL, target)

    assert _names_in(tmp_path) == {"Q.pdf"}
@

The temporary file must not change which formats work, so every format
[[segno]] accepts together with a scale gives what [[segno]] itself would write.
[[segno]] cannot write all of them to a stream, which is why the temporary file
is a path and not a buffer.  The clock is fixed because some of them hold a
date.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    "fmt", ["png", "svg", "eps", "tex", "xbm", "xpm", "pbm", "pam", "ppm"]
)
def test_make_qr_writes_every_format_as_segno_does(
    tmp_path, monkeypatch, fmt
):
    monkeypatch.setattr("time.strftime", lambda *_args: "20300101000000")
    direct = tmp_path / f"direct.{fmt}"
    segno.make(URL, error=QR_ERROR_LEVEL).save(direct, scale=QR_SCALE)

    written = make_qr(URL, tmp_path / f"Q.{fmt}", fmt=fmt)

    assert written.read_bytes() == direct.read_bytes()
@

The sidecar is the heart of the anti-drift contract, so the contents matter.
We assert that PDF output yields a sidecar that points at the QR by basename
and that the URL appears in the caption.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_writes_pdf_tex_sidecar_with_the_url(tmp_path):
    output = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")
    sidecar = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex")

    contents = sidecar.read_text()
    expected_include = (
        f"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{{{output.as_posix()}}}"
    )
    assert expected_include in contents
    assert "http://localhost:8080/abcd" in contents
@

The caption goes through the [[\mentipyqrcaption]] hook, and the sidecar
carries the hook's default so an unprepared document still compiles.  Pinning
both halves keeps a refactor from inlining the caption again, which would
silently take the formatting knob away from documents that rely on it.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_sidecar_routes_the_caption_through_a_hook(tmp_path):
    output = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")

    contents = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex").read_text()
    assert "\\mentipyqrcaption{http://localhost:8080/abcd}" in contents
    assert "\\providecommand{\\mentipyqrcaption}[1]" in contents
@

PNG output is used both for the HTML results page and for the printed manual
(PythonTeX includes PNGs via [[\includegraphics]] just as readily as PDFs).
The sidecar therefore points at whichever image file [[make_qr]] just wrote,
not unconditionally at a PDF.  This test pins that the sidecar follows the
chosen format.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_sidecar_references_the_chosen_format(tmp_path):
    output = make_qr(
        "http://localhost:8080/abcd",
        tmp_path / "Q-abcd.png",
        fmt="png",
    )
    sidecar = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex")

    assert sidecar.exists()
    contents = sidecar.read_text()
    expected_include = (
        f"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{{{output.as_posix()}}}"
    )
    assert expected_include in contents
@

Real URLs carry characters that LaTeX text mode would otherwise mangle.  We
verify that underscores, ampersands, percent signs, and hashes survive into
the sidecar in their escaped form, so the caption typesets verbatim.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_sidecar_escapes_special_chars_in_url(tmp_path):
    url = "http://example.test/poll_42?lang=en&debug=1#answer"
    output = make_qr(url, tmp_path / "Q-x.pdf")

    contents = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex").read_text()
    assert "poll\\_42" in contents
    assert "lang=en\\&debug=1" in contents
    assert "\\#answer" in contents
@

Runtime QR regeneration writes to an absolute path, but the document should
keep including the relative path it was compiled with.  This test fixes that
distinction so a future refactor does not collapse the two path roles.

<<test functions>>=
def test_make_qr_sidecar_can_keep_a_relative_include_path(tmp_path):
    qr_subpath = Path(DEFAULT_QR_DIR).as_posix()
    output = make_qr(
        "http://localhost:8080/abcd",
        tmp_path / "slides" / "Q-abcd.pdf",
        include_path=Path(DEFAULT_QR_DIR) / "Q-abcd.pdf",
    )

    contents = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex").read_text()
    assert (
        f"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{{{qr_subpath}/Q-abcd.pdf}}"
        in contents
    )
@

A sidecar keeps its date only when its whole text would stay the same.  Any
difference counts: another address, another include path, or a layout from an
earlier release.  One that cannot be read is overwritten rather than an error,
since nothing in it is worth keeping.  The tests date the file back, because two
writes within one test can share a timestamp.

<<test functions>>=
def _sidecar_dated_back(tmp_path):
    output = make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")
    sidecar = output.with_suffix(".pdf_tex")
    os.utime(sidecar, (1_000_000_000, 1_000_000_000))
    return sidecar, sidecar.stat().st_mtime_ns


def test_make_qr_keeps_the_date_of_a_sidecar_that_would_not_change(tmp_path):
    sidecar, dated = _sidecar_dated_back(tmp_path)

    make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")

    assert sidecar.stat().st_mtime_ns == dated


@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    "url, include_path",
    [
        ("http://other.example:8080/abcd", None),
        ("http://localhost:8080/abcd", "mentipy-obj/Q-abcd.pdf"),
    ],
    ids=["address", "include-path"],
)
def test_make_qr_rewrites_a_sidecar_that_changes(tmp_path, url, include_path):
    sidecar, dated = _sidecar_dated_back(tmp_path)

    make_qr(url, tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf", include_path=include_path)

    assert sidecar.stat().st_mtime_ns != dated
    assert (include_path or url) in sidecar.read_text()


@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    "earlier",
    [b"\\mentipyqrcaption{http://localhost:8080/abcd}\n", b"\xff\xfe"],
    ids=["other-layout", "unreadable"],
)
def test_make_qr_replaces_any_other_sidecar_at_the_same_address(
    tmp_path, earlier
):
    sidecar, _dated = _sidecar_dated_back(tmp_path)
    sidecar.write_bytes(earlier)

    make_qr("http://localhost:8080/abcd", tmp_path / "Q-abcd.pdf")

    assert "\\includegraphics" in sidecar.read_text()
@

The recorded include path must survive a round trip through the
sidecar, and a missing sidecar must say so rather than invent a path.

